Similarly, dish retrieval (G2) is designed with a fallback (OR) operator to allow for multiple operational modes: unassisted (G2.1), robot cooperation (G2.2), or human assistance (G2.3). Lastly, door opening (G3) also uses a fallback (OR) operator to cover both robot-cooperative and human-cooperative methods.

Each goal’s tasks are specified with sequential (“;”) execution where the order is critical (e.g., signaling, guiding, confirming for tray retrieval) or as leaf tasks (indicated by “‐”) when they represent direct actions. The number of robots required for each task is determined by the nature of the action (e.g., table delivery and dish retrieval unassisted require one robot; coordinated actions require two).

This detailed hierarchical decomposition provides clear guidance for a multi-robot system to execute the mission while handling uncertainties (e.g., availability of human assistance or inpatient ability) and ensuring proper tracking and error management.

